INT. MUNICIPAL PLANETARIUM - CONTROL ROOM - NIGHT

A cramped booth above the dome. Dials, a projector the size of an oven, a dead cup of coffee on every flat surface.

JUNIPER "JUNE" ASANTE (30s, hoodie, headset, a laptop plastered with stickers) sits at a console, bored. Beside her, WALLACE PYO (60s, security guard uniform one size small, a thermos) pretends to read a paperback.

Through the glass, the empty dome is lit with a slow field of artificial stars.

WALLACE
You're not supposed to be up here.

JUNE
Maintenance.

WALLACE
At two a.m.?

JUNE
Stars don't recalibrate themselves.

He sips from the thermos. Turns a page without reading it.

JUNE (CONT'D)
(into headset, flat)
Ceiling's clear. Roof hatch in four.

WALLACE
Who're you talking to?

JUNE
Myself. Old habit.

WALLACE
You said "roof hatch."

JUNE
I said "roof catch." As in, catch up on emails.

WALLACE
Mm.

He doesn't believe her. He doesn't move, either.

June taps a key. The stars shift. The constellation of Orion slides west.

WALLACE (CONT'D)
My wife loved Orion.

JUNE
Everyone does.

WALLACE
She said it looked like a man waiting for a bus.

June laughs. Real. Surprised.

JUNE
That's good.

WALLACE
She was good.

Quiet. The projector whirs.

JUNE
(under her breath, to the headset)
Hold. Hold. He's talking.

WALLACE
There it is again.

JUNE
Hold on, I'm - (beat) - praying. Quietly. For my laptop. It crashes.

WALLACE
Sure it does.

He sets down the paperback, stands, and stretches until his back cracks.

WALLACE (CONT'D)
(to the empty booth)
Everyone out of the vault when you're done, please. I lock at six.

Beat. June looks at him.

JUNE
You knew.

WALLACE
I'm sixty-one. I know what a van looks like parked behind a dome at midnight.

JUNE
And you sat here.

WALLACE
I'm on break.

He pours coffee. Offers her the cup.

She takes it.

WALLACE (CONT'D)
Don't take the telescope. It's on loan from the university.

JUNE
(into headset)
Hear that, everybody? Leave the telescope.

Overhead, the stars drift on, indifferent.
